<Layout title="How to Make a Resume Easy for ATS Software to Read" active="guides"><main id="main" class="content-page article"><a href="/guides/">← All career guides</a><h1 style="margin-top:30px">Make your resume easy to read—by people and software.</h1><p class="lead">An applicant tracking system helps an employer manage applications. Clear formatting helps software extract information, but readability is different from being qualified for a job.</p><h2>Start with structure</h2><p>Use recognizable section headings: Experience, Education, Skills and Projects. A single-column layout gives information a straightforward reading order. Keep important details in the main document, not inside an image.</p><h2>Export actual text</h2><p>A PDF made from screenshots may look good but contain no selectable text. GlobalCV creates text-based PDFs. After downloading, try selecting and copying a paragraph to check that it reads sensibly.</p><h2>Use the job description honestly</h2><p>Look for the skills, qualifications and responsibilities the employer asks for. Include those you genuinely have and support them with evidence. Repeating a keyword many times is not a substitute for relevant experience.</p><h2>Do not chase a universal score</h2><p>A formatting check can identify issues in a document. A job comparison can point out differences between your experience and a vacancy. Neither predicts the employer’s decision or provides a universal score across recruiting systems.</p><h2>A quick final check</h2><ul><li>Your name and contact details are easy to find.</li><li>Dates and job titles are consistent.</li><li>Sections follow a sensible reading order.</li><li>The PDF contains selectable text.</li><li>You have followed the employer’s requested format.</li></ul><p>Reference: <a href="https://cloudfront.careeronestop.org/JobSearch/Resumes/ResumeGuide/formatting.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CareerOneStop’s formatting guidance</a>.</p><a href="/templates/" class="button primary">Choose a clear template →</a></main></Layout>
